-- title:
--   Lodha–Moore: G₀ = ⟨a, b, c⟩, the functions x, y of binary sequences, the relations R and R₀, K, and μ-amenable relations
-- statement:
--   Notions of Lodha and Moore's §1–§3 (pp. 2–7).
--
--   **The group $G_0$ (p. 2).** p. 2: “The group is generated by $a(t) = t + 1$ together with the following two homeomorphisms of $\mathbb R$: $b(t) = \begin{cases} t & \text{if } t \le 0 \\ \frac{t}{1-t} & \text{if } 0 \le t \le \frac12 \\ 3 - \frac1t & \text{if } \frac12 \le t \le 1 \\ t + 1 & \text{if } 1 \le t \end{cases} \qquad c(t) = \begin{cases} \frac{2t}{1+t} & \text{if } 0 \le t \le 1 \\ t & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}$” `aFun`, `bFun`, `cFun` are these three functions $a$, $b$, $c$. `a`, `b`, `c` (`ofReal`) are the homeomorphisms of the projective line $\mathbb R \cup \{\infty\}$ that agree with them on $\mathbb R$ (the identity if there were none; the milestones show there is one). p. 2, Theorem 1.1: “The group $G_0$ generated by the functions $a(t)$, $b(t)$, and $c(t)$ is nonamenable and finitely presented.” `G0` is the group they generate inside the homeomorphisms of the projective line, the ambient group of Monod's $H$.
--
--   **Binary sequences (pp. 2–3).** p. 2: “Let $2^{\mathbb N}$ denote the collection of all infinite binary sequences and let $2^{<\mathbb N}$ denote the collection of all finite binary sequences.” `Seq` is a finite binary sequence (`false` for 0); infinite sequences are `Stream' Bool`. p. 2: “If $s$ and $t$ are finite binary sequences, then we will write $s \subseteq t$ if $s$ is an initial segment of $t$ and $s \subset t$ if $s$ is a proper initial segment of $t$. If neither $s \subseteq t$ nor $t \subseteq s$, then we will say that $s$ and $t$ are *incompatible*.” `Incompatible s t`: neither is an initial segment of the other. `IsConst s`: all digits of $s$ are equal (the empty sequence is constant); the source speaks of a constant finite sequence (p. 6) without a defining sentence. pp. 2–3: “The set $2^{<\mathbb N}$ is equipped with a lexicographic order defined by $s <_{\mathrm{lex}} t$ if $t \subset s$ or $s$ and $t$ are incompatible and $s(i) < t(i)$ where $i$ is minimal such that $s(i) \neq t(i)$.” `LexLt` is this order, in which a proper initial segment comes *after* its extensions. `EventuallyConst ξ`: $\xi$ is constant from some point on; the source uses the term (p. 3) without a defining sentence.
--
--   **Continued fractions (p. 3).** p. 3: “Define a map $\Phi : 2^{\mathbb N} \to \mathbb R \cup \{\infty\}$ as follows. First define $\phi : 2^{\mathbb N} \to [0, \infty]$ by $\phi(0\xi) = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{\phi(\xi)}} \qquad \phi(1\xi) = 1 + \phi(\xi)$ and set $\Phi(0\xi) = -\phi(\tilde\xi) \qquad \Phi(1\xi) = \phi(\xi).$” `cfStep` is $t \mapsto 1/(1 + 1/t)$ for the digit 0 and $t \mapsto 1 + t$ for the digit 1, on $[0, \infty]$. `phi` is $\phi$, defined as the limit of the composites of `cfStep` along the first $n$ digits, evaluated at 1; Lodha and Moore define $\phi$ by the two equations, which the milestone `phi_cons_and_eq_phi_of_cons_and_Phi_fibers` states, together with the fact that $\phi$ is the only function satisfying them. `Phi` is $\Phi$, with $\tilde\xi$ the complement of $\xi$; p. 3: “Given an infinite sequence $s$, $\tilde s$ is the sequence satisfying $\tilde s(i) = 0$ if $s(i) = 1$ and $\tilde s(i) = 1$ if $s(i) = 0$.”
--
--   **The functions $x$ and $y$ (p. 5).** p. 5: “We start with the following two primitive functions defined on binary sequences: $\xi.x = \begin{cases} 0\eta & \text{if } \xi = 00\eta \\ 10\eta & \text{if } \xi = 01\eta \\ 11\eta & \text{if } \xi = 1\eta \end{cases} \qquad \xi.y = \begin{cases} 0(\eta.y) & \text{if } \xi = 00\eta \\ 10(\eta.y^{-1}) & \text{if } \xi = 01\eta \\ 11(\eta.y) & \text{if } \xi = 1\eta \end{cases}$” `xFun` is $x$. `yFun true` is $y$ and `yFun false` is $y^{-1}$, by the recursion for $y$ above together with $y^{-1}$: $0\eta \mapsto 00(\eta.y^{-1})$, $10\eta \mapsto 01(\eta.y)$, $11\eta \mapsto 1(\eta.y^{-1})$ (`yStep`, `yOut`: digit $n$ of the output is read off after $n + 1$ steps, each of which outputs at least one digit). The rules for $y^{-1}$ are those of Definition 5.7. p. 5: “From these functions, we define families of functions $x_s$ ($s \in 2^{<\mathbb N}$) and $y_s$ ($s \in 2^{<\mathbb N}$) which act just as $x$ and $y$, but localized to those binary sequences which extend $s$. $\xi.x_s = \begin{cases} s^\frown(\eta.x) & \text{if } \xi = s^\frown\eta \\ \xi & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \qquad \xi.y_s = \begin{cases} s^\frown(\eta.y) & \text{if } \xi = s^\frown\eta \\ \xi & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}$” `localize s f` acts as $f$ on the sequences extending $s$ and fixes the others; `xSeq s`, `ySeq s` are $x_s$, $y_s$.
--
--   **The groups (p. 6).** p. 3: “We will follow [4] and write $s.f$ for the result of applying an automorphism $f$ to the input $s$. The operation of $F$ is therefore defined as $s.(fg) = (s.f).g$.” Products are therefore left to right, and `SeqGroup` is the opposite of the group of permutations of infinite binary sequences, whose product is "$f$ followed by $g$". `toSeqGroup f` is $f$ as an element of it (the identity if $f$ were not a bijection), and `xs s`, `ys s` are $x_s$, $y_s$. p. 6: “We will use $F$ to denote the group generated by $\{x_s : s \in 2^{<\mathbb N}\}$.” p. 6: “Define $X = \{x_s : s \in 2^{<\mathbb N}\}$, $Y = \{y_s : s \in 2^{<\mathbb N}\}$, and $Y_0$ to be the set of all $y_s$ such that $s$ is not a constant binary sequence.” p. 6: “The group $G_0$ defined in the introduction is therefore generated by the (redundant) generating set $S_0 = X \cup Y_0$.” p. 6: “Let $R_0$ be those relations in $R$ which only refer to generators in $S_0$ and let $G$ be the group generated by $S = X \cup Y$.” `F`, `G`, `G0Seq` are the subgroups generated by $X$, by $S = X \cup Y$, and by $S_0 = X \cup Y_0$.
--
--   **Relations (p. 6).** p. 6: “It is straightforward to verify that the following relations are satisfied by these elements, where $s$ and $t$ are finite binary sequences: (1) $x_s^2 = x_{s0}x_sx_{s1}$; (2) if $t.x_s$ is defined, then $x_tx_s = x_sx_{t.x_s}$; (3) if $t.x_s$ is defined, then $y_tx_s = x_sy_{t.x_s}$; (4) if $s$ and $t$ are incompatible, then $y_sy_t = y_ty_s$; (5) $y_s = x_sy_{s0}y_{s10}^{-1}y_{s11}$. We will refer to these relations collectively as $R$.” The source defines “$t.x_s$ is defined” only through the map of a tree diagram $(L, R)$, p. 3: “A tree diagram describes a map of infinite binary sequences as follows: $s_i{}^\frown\xi \mapsto t_i{}^\frown\xi$ where $s_i$ and $t_i$ are the $i$th elements of $L$ and $R$ respectively and $\xi$ is any binary sequence.” and “This map is also defined on any finite binary sequence $u$ such that $u$ has a prefix in $L$.” `xFin s t` is $t.x_s$ on finite sequences: $s00u \mapsto s0u$, $s01u \mapsto s10u$, $s1u \mapsto s11u$, and $t$ itself when $t$ is incompatible with $s$; undefined when $t$ is an initial segment of $s$ or equals $s0$. `Gen` has a generator $x_s$ and a generator $y_s$ for each finite sequence. `Rel` has one constructor for each of the families (1)–(5), with its two sides (`Rel.lhs`, `Rel.rhs`, words multiplied left to right) and the generators it refers to (`Rel.gens`). `R` is the set of relators `lhs · rhs⁻¹`; `R0` (the $R_0$ of the p. 6 sentence quoted above) the relators of the relations all of whose generators lie in $S_0$ (`Gen.InS0`), and `R0S` the same relators in the generators of $S_0$ alone. `RX` is the relations (1) and (2) in the generators $x_s$ alone. `FinAct g s t` is $s.g = t$: $g$ maps $s^\frown\xi$ to $t^\frown\xi$ for every infinite $\xi$.
--
--   **The nine relations (p. 7).** p. 7: “In the case of $G_0$, one can check that the following list of 9 relations actually suffice: $x_1x^{-2}x_1x = x^{-1}x_1xx_1x^{-1} \qquad x_1x^{-3}x_1x^2 = x^{-2}x_1x^2x_1x^{-1} \qquad y_{10}x_0 = x_0y_{10} \qquad y_{10}x_{01} = x_{01}y_{10} \qquad y_{10}x_{11} = x_{11}y_{10} \qquad y_{10}x_{111} = x_{111}y_{10} \qquad y_{01}y_{10} = y_{10}y_{01} \qquad y_{001}y_{10} = y_{10}y_{001} \qquad y_{10} = x_{10}y_{100}y_{1010}^{-1}y_{1011}.$” After a parenthetical remark, p. 7: “When expressed in terms of the original generators, these become: $ba^{-2}ba = a^{-1}baba^{-1} \qquad ba^{-1}a^{-2}ba^2 = a^{-2}ba^2ba^{-1} \qquad ca^2b^{-1}a^{-1} = a^2b^{-1}a^{-1}c \qquad cb^2a^{-1}bab = b^2a^{-1}babc \qquad ca^{-1}ba = a^{-1}bac \qquad ca^{-2}ba^2 = a^{-2}ba^2c \qquad caca^{-1} = aca^{-1}c \qquad ca^2ca^{-2} = a^2ca^{-2}c \qquad c = b^2a^{-1}b^{-1}acb^{-2}ab^{-1}c^{-1}ba^{-1}bab^{-1}ab^{-1}cba^{-1}ba^{-1}.$” `nineRels` are the nine relators in $a$, $b$, $c$ (words multiplied left to right). The first three and the fifth to eighth are as printed. The fourth and ninth are the translations of $y_{10}x_{01} = x_{01}y_{10}$ and $y_{10} = x_{10}y_{100}y_{1010}^{-1}y_{1011}$ through the definitions of p. 6, with $x_{10} = x_1^2x^{-1}x_1^{-1}xx_1^{-1}$ and $x_s = f_s^{-1}x_{10}f_s$, $y_s = f_s^{-1}y_{10}f_s$ for $f_{01} = x^{-1}$, $f_{100} = x_1^{-1}$, $f_{1010} = x_1^{-1}xx_1^{-1}$, $f_{1011} = xx_1^{-1}xx_1^{-1}$: $c \cdot ab^2a^{-1}b^{-1}ab^{-1}a^{-1} = ab^2a^{-1}b^{-1}ab^{-1}a^{-1} \cdot c$ and $c = b^2a^{-1}b^{-1}aca^{-1}bc^{-1}a^{-1}cab^{-1}ab^{-1}$.
--
--   **$K$ (p. 4).** p. 4: “Let $K$ denote the subgroup of $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)$ generated by the matrices $\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix} \quad \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix} \quad \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt2&0\\0&\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\end{pmatrix}.$” `kTrans`, `kInv`, `kDil` are these three matrices in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb R)$ (written over the subring $\top = \mathbb R$, so that Monod's Möbius action `Monod.mob` applies), and `K` the subgroup of $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)$ (`Matrix.ProjectiveSpecialLinearGroup`, the quotient of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb R)$ by $\pm 1$) generated by their classes. A class acts on the projective line by the Möbius map of either of its matrices.
--
--   **$\mu$-amenable relations (p. 4).** p. 4: “Let $X$ be a Polish space and let $E \subseteq X^2$ be an equivalence relation which is Borel and which has countable equivalence classes. $E$ is *$\mu$-amenable* if, after discarding a $\mu$-measure 0 set, $E$ is the orbit equivalence relation of an action of $\mathbb Z$. (This is not the standard definition, but it is equivalent by [7].)” `IsMuAmenable μ E`: there is a measurable $\mu$-null set $N$ and a Borel automorphism $T$ of $X \setminus N$ such that two points of $X \setminus N$ are $E$-related exactly when one is $T^n$ of the other for some $n \in \mathbb Z$.
--
--   **Formalization Note (pp. 3, 6).** Group products are left to right, as in the paper (p. 3), both in `SeqGroup` and, through the opposite group, in the statements about $a$, $b$, $c$; Monod's group of homeomorphisms composes right to left.
--
--   **Formalization Note (p. 7).** As printed, the fourth relation $cb^2a^{-1}bab = b^2a^{-1}babc$ and the ninth $c = b^2a^{-1}b^{-1}acb^{-2}ab^{-1}c^{-1}ba^{-1}bab^{-1}ab^{-1}cba^{-1}ba^{-1}$ do not hold in $G_0$: each side acts differently at, for instance, $t = 29/390$. The relations in $x_s$, $y_s$ that they translate do hold, and `nineRels` uses their translations. That the printed fourth and ninth relations fail is proved on the platform: [`LodhaMoorePrinted.printed_relations_four_and_nine_ne`](https://prove2.me/theorems/b8f729b4-c077-4b08-91bc-5999cf6f5e71).
--
--   **Formalization Note (p. 4).** Lodha and Moore's definition of a $\mu$-amenable relation does not say that the action of $\mathbb Z$ is Borel. Read without that, every equivalence relation with countable classes is the orbit relation of an action of $\mathbb Z$ (arrange each class as a cycle or as a copy of $\mathbb Z$), so every such relation would be $\mu$-amenable and Theorem 2.2 would be false. That every equivalence relation with countable classes is the orbit relation of a single permutation is proved on the platform: [`CountableOrbit.exists_perm_rel_iff_exists_zpow_eq`](https://prove2.me/theorems/506d353f-577e-4bdb-825b-42df371db067). The definition here takes the action to be by a Borel automorphism, the standard reading under which it is equivalent to amenability, as their parenthetical “This is not the standard definition, but it is equivalent by [7].” asserts; that equivalence is stated as two milestones.
-- source:
--   Lodha, Y. and Moore, J. T., A nonamenable finitely presented group of piecewise projective homeomorphisms, Groups Geom. Dyn. 10 (2016) 177–200, https://doi.org/10.4171/GGD/347 (arXiv:1308.4250v3, whose page numbers are used), pp. 2–7, §1–§3 (a, b, c, G₀, Φ, x, y, x_s, y_s, R, R₀, K, μ-amenable)

import Mathlib

/-!
# Lodha–Moore, *A nonamenable finitely presented group of piecewise projective homeomorphisms*

Y. Lodha and J. T. Moore, Groups Geom. Dyn. 10 (2016) 177–200, arXiv:1308.4250v3 (whose page
numbers are used): the homeomorphisms `a`, `b`, `c` of the line and the group `G0` (p. 2), and the
functions `x`, `y` of binary sequences, their localizations `x_s`, `y_s`, and the groups they
generate (pp. 3, 5–6).
-/

namespace LodhaMoore

open MulOpposite

/-! ### The homeomorphisms `a`, `b`, `c` of the line (p. 2) -/

/-- `a(t) = t + 1` (p. 2). -/
noncomputable def aFun (t : ℝ) : ℝ := t + 1

/-- `b(t)` (p. 2): `t` for `t ≤ 0`, `t / (1 - t)` for `0 ≤ t ≤ 1/2`, `3 - 1/t` for `1/2 ≤ t ≤ 1`,
`t + 1` for `1 ≤ t`. -/
noncomputable def bFun (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if t ≤ 0 then t else if t ≤ 1 / 2 then t / (1 - t) else if t ≤ 1 then 3 - 1 / t else t + 1

/-- `c(t)` (p. 2): `2t / (1 + t)` for `0 ≤ t ≤ 1`, `t` otherwise. -/
noncomputable def cFun (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if 0 ≤ t ∧ t ≤ 1 then 2 * t / (1 + t) else t

open Classical in
/-- The homeomorphism of the projective line `ℝ ∪ {∞}` that agrees with `f` on `ℝ`, if there is
one, and the identity otherwise. -/
noncomputable def ofReal (f : ℝ → ℝ) : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ :=
  if h : ∃ e : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ, ∀ t : ℝ, e t = ((f t : ℝ) : OnePoint ℝ) then h.choose
  else Homeomorph.refl _

/-- `a` as a homeomorphism of the projective line. -/
noncomputable def a : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ := ofReal aFun

/-- `b` as a homeomorphism of the projective line. -/
noncomputable def b : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ := ofReal bFun

/-- `c` as a homeomorphism of the projective line. -/
noncomputable def c : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ := ofReal cFun

/-- Theorem 1.1's `G0`: the group generated by `a`, `b`, `c`. -/
def G0 : Subgroup (OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ) := Subgroup.closure {a, b, c}

/-! ### Binary sequences (pp. 2–3) -/

/-- A finite binary sequence (`false` for `0`, `true` for `1`). -/
abbrev Seq := List Bool

/-- `s` and `t` are **incompatible**: neither is an initial segment of the other (p. 2). -/
def Incompatible (s t : Seq) : Prop := ¬ s <+: t ∧ ¬ t <+: s

/-- `s` is a **constant** finite binary sequence: all its digits are equal. -/
def IsConst (s : Seq) : Prop := ∀ i < s.length, ∀ j < s.length, s[i]? = s[j]?

/-! ### The functions `x` and `y` of infinite binary sequences (p. 5) -/

/-- `x` (p. 5): `00η ↦ 0η`, `01η ↦ 10η`, `1η ↦ 11η`. -/
def xFun (ξ : Stream' Bool) : Stream' Bool :=
  match ξ 0, ξ 1 with
  | false, false => ξ.tail
  | false, true => Stream'.cons true (Stream'.cons false (ξ.drop 2))
  | true, _ => Stream'.cons true ξ

/-- One step of the recursion defining `y` (`σ = true`) and `y⁻¹` (`σ = false`) on infinite
sequences (p. 5): the output digits of the step, the next state, and the rest of the input.
`y`: `00η ↦ 0 (η.y)`, `01η ↦ 10 (η.y⁻¹)`, `1η ↦ 11 (η.y)`;
`y⁻¹`: `0η ↦ 00 (η.y⁻¹)`, `10η ↦ 01 (η.y)`, `11η ↦ 1 (η.y⁻¹)`. -/
def yStep : Bool → Stream' Bool → Seq × Bool × Stream' Bool
  | true, ξ =>
    match ξ 0, ξ 1 with
    | false, false => ([false], true, ξ.drop 2)
    | false, true => ([true, false], false, ξ.drop 2)
    | true, _ => ([true, true], true, ξ.drop 1)
  | false, ξ =>
    match ξ 0, ξ 1 with
    | false, _ => ([false, false], false, ξ.drop 1)
    | true, false => ([false, true], true, ξ.drop 2)
    | true, true => ([true], false, ξ.drop 2)

/-- The output of the first `k` steps of the recursion from state `σ`. -/
def yOut : ℕ → Bool → Stream' Bool → Seq
  | 0, _, _ => []
  | k + 1, σ, ξ => (yStep σ ξ).1 ++ yOut k (yStep σ ξ).2.1 (yStep σ ξ).2.2

/-- `y` (`σ = true`) and `y⁻¹` (`σ = false`) on infinite sequences: digit `n` of the output is
digit `n` of the output of the first `n + 1` steps of the recursion. -/
def yFun (σ : Bool) (ξ : Stream' Bool) : Stream' Bool :=
  fun n => (yOut (n + 1) σ ξ).getD n false

/-- `f` localized to the sequences extending `s` (p. 5): `s⌢η ↦ s⌢(η.f)`, other sequences fixed. -/
def localize (s : Seq) (f : Stream' Bool → Stream' Bool) (ξ : Stream' Bool) : Stream' Bool :=
  if ξ.take s.length = s then s ++ₛ f (ξ.drop s.length) else ξ

/-- `x_s` as a function of infinite sequences (p. 5). -/
def xSeq (s : Seq) : Stream' Bool → Stream' Bool := localize s xFun

/-- `y_s` as a function of infinite sequences (p. 5). -/
def ySeq (s : Seq) : Stream' Bool → Stream' Bool := localize s (yFun true)

/-! ### The groups (p. 6)

Lodha and Moore write `ξ.f` for the image of `ξ` under `f` and compose left to right:
`ξ.(fg) = (ξ.f).g` (p. 3). The groups below are subgroups of the opposite of the permutation group
of infinite binary sequences, whose product is "`f` followed by `g`". -/

/-- The permutations of infinite binary sequences with the product `f · g = g ∘ f`. -/
abbrev SeqGroup := (Equiv.Perm (Stream' Bool))ᵐᵒᵖ

open Classical in
/-- `f` as an element of `SeqGroup` when it is a bijection, and the identity otherwise. -/
noncomputable def toSeqGroup (f : Stream' Bool → Stream' Bool) : SeqGroup :=
  if h : Function.Bijective f then op (Equiv.ofBijective f h) else 1

/-- `x_s` as an element of `SeqGroup`. -/
noncomputable def xs (s : Seq) : SeqGroup := toSeqGroup (xSeq s)

/-- `y_s` as an element of `SeqGroup`. -/
noncomputable def ys (s : Seq) : SeqGroup := toSeqGroup (ySeq s)

/-- `F`: the group generated by `X = {x_s}` (p. 6). -/
def F : Subgroup SeqGroup := Subgroup.closure (Set.range xs)

/-- `G`: the group generated by `S = X ∪ Y` (p. 6). -/
def G : Subgroup SeqGroup := Subgroup.closure (Set.range xs ∪ Set.range ys)

/-- `G0` on sequences: the group generated by `S0 = X ∪ Y0`, `Y0` the `y_s` with `s` not
constant (p. 6). -/
def G0Seq : Subgroup SeqGroup := Subgroup.closure (Set.range xs ∪ ys '' {s | ¬ IsConst s})

/-! ### The partial action of `x_s` on finite sequences, and the relations `R` (p. 6) -/

/-- `t.x_s` for a finite sequence `t` (pp. 3, 5): `s⌢00u ↦ s⌢0u`, `s⌢01u ↦ s⌢10u`,
`s⌢1u ↦ s⌢11u`; `t` itself when `t` is incompatible with `s`; undefined (`none`) when `t` is an
initial segment of `s` or equals `s⌢0`. -/
def xFin (s t : Seq) : Option Seq :=
  if s <+: t then
    match t.drop s.length with
    | false :: false :: r => some (s ++ false :: r)
    | false :: true :: r => some (s ++ true :: false :: r)
    | true :: r => some (s ++ true :: true :: r)
    | _ => none
  else if t <+: s then none else some t

/-- The generators: `x s` stands for `x_s` and `y s` for `y_s`. -/
inductive Gen
  | x (s : Seq)
  | y (s : Seq)
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- The value of a generator in `SeqGroup`. -/
noncomputable def Gen.val : Gen → SeqGroup
  | .x s => xs s
  | .y s => ys s

/-- `S0 = X ∪ Y0` (p. 6): the generators `x_s`, and the `y_s` with `s` not constant. -/
def Gen.InS0 : Gen → Prop
  | .x _ => True
  | .y s => ¬ IsConst s

/-- The relations `R` (p. 6), one constructor for each of the five families:
(1) `x_s² = x_{s0} x_s x_{s1}`; (2) if `t.x_s` is defined, `x_t x_s = x_s x_{t.x_s}`;
(3) if `t.x_s` is defined, `y_t x_s = x_s y_{t.x_s}`; (4) if `s`, `t` are incompatible,
`y_s y_t = y_t y_s`; (5) `y_s = x_s y_{s0} y_{s10}⁻¹ y_{s11}`. -/
inductive Rel
  | one (s : Seq)
  | two (s t t' : Seq) (h : xFin s t = some t')
  | three (s t t' : Seq) (h : xFin s t = some t')
  | four (s t : Seq) (h : Incompatible s t)
  | five (s : Seq)

open FreeGroup in
/-- The left side of a relation, as a word in the generators, multiplied left to right. -/
def Rel.lhs : Rel → FreeGroup Gen
  | .one s => of (.x s) * of (.x s)
  | .two s t _ _ => of (.x t) * of (.x s)
  | .three s t _ _ => of (.y t) * of (.x s)
  | .four s t _ => of (.y s) * of (.y t)
  | .five s => of (.y s)

open FreeGroup in
/-- The right side of a relation. -/
def Rel.rhs : Rel → FreeGroup Gen
  | .one s => of (.x (s ++ [false])) * of (.x s) * of (.x (s ++ [true]))
  | .two s _ t' _ => of (.x s) * of (.x t')
  | .three s _ t' _ => of (.x s) * of (.y t')
  | .four s t _ => of (.y t) * of (.y s)
  | .five s => of (.x s) * of (.y (s ++ [false])) * (of (.y (s ++ [true, false])))⁻¹ *
      of (.y (s ++ [true, true]))

/-- The generators a relation refers to. -/
def Rel.gens : Rel → List Gen
  | .one s => [.x s, .x (s ++ [false]), .x (s ++ [true])]
  | .two s t t' _ => [.x t, .x s, .x t']
  | .three s t t' _ => [.y t, .x s, .y t']
  | .four s t _ => [.y s, .y t]
  | .five s => [.y s, .x s, .y (s ++ [false]), .y (s ++ [true, false]), .y (s ++ [true, true])]

/-- `R` as a set of relators `lhs · rhs⁻¹` (p. 6). -/
def R : Set (FreeGroup Gen) := Set.range fun r : Rel => r.lhs * r.rhs⁻¹

/-- `R0`: the relations in `R` which only refer to generators in `S0` (p. 6). -/
def R0 : Set (FreeGroup Gen) := (fun r : Rel => r.lhs * r.rhs⁻¹) '' {r | ∀ g ∈ r.gens, g.InS0}

/-! ### Continued fractions: `phi` and `Phi` (p. 3) -/

/-- `t ↦ 1 / (1 + 1/t)` (digit `0`) and `t ↦ 1 + t` (digit `1`) on `[0, ∞]`. -/
noncomputable def cfStep (d : Bool) (t : ENNReal) : ENNReal := if d then 1 + t else (1 + t⁻¹)⁻¹

/-- The value at `1` of the composite of the maps of the first `n` digits of `ξ`. -/
noncomputable def cfApprox (ξ : Stream' Bool) (n : ℕ) : ENNReal :=
  ((List.range n).map ξ).foldr cfStep 1

/-- `phi : 2^ℕ → [0, ∞]` (p. 3), as the limit of the composites
`cfStep ξ(0) ∘ ⋯ ∘ cfStep ξ(n-1)`, so that `phi(0ξ) = 1 / (1 + 1/phi(ξ))` and `phi(1ξ) = 1 + phi(ξ)`. -/
noncomputable def phi (ξ : Stream' Bool) : ENNReal := Filter.limUnder Filter.atTop (cfApprox ξ)

/-- A point of `[0, ∞]` as a point of `ℝ ∪ {∞}`, `∞ ↦ ∞`. -/
noncomputable def toP1 (t : ENNReal) : OnePoint ℝ := if t = ⊤ then OnePoint.infty else (t.toReal : ℝ)

/-- `Phi : 2^ℕ → ℝ ∪ {∞}` (p. 3): `Phi(0ξ) = -phi(ξ̃)`, `Phi(1ξ) = phi(ξ)`, `ξ̃` the complement of `ξ`. -/
noncomputable def Phi (ξ : Stream' Bool) : OnePoint ℝ :=
  if ξ 0 then toP1 (phi ξ.tail)
  else if phi (fun n => !ξ.tail n) = ⊤ then OnePoint.infty
  else ((-(phi (fun n => !ξ.tail n)).toReal : ℝ) : OnePoint ℝ)

/-! ### The group `K` (p. 4) -/

/-- `[[1, 1], [0, 1]]`, the map `t ↦ t + 1`. -/
noncomputable def kTrans : Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) (⊤ : Subring ℝ) :=
  ⟨!![1, 1; 0, 1], by simp [Matrix.det_fin_two]⟩

/-- `[[0, 1], [-1, 0]]`, the map `t ↦ -1/t`. -/
noncomputable def kInv : Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) (⊤ : Subring ℝ) :=
  ⟨!![0, 1; -1, 0], by simp [Matrix.det_fin_two]⟩

/-- `[[√2, 0], [0, 1/√2]]`, the map `t ↦ 2t`. -/
noncomputable def kDil : Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) (⊤ : Subring ℝ) :=
  ⟨!![⟨Real.sqrt 2, trivial⟩, 0; 0, ⟨1 / Real.sqrt 2, trivial⟩], by
    simp only [Matrix.det_fin_two_of]
    ext
    simp⟩

/-- `K` (p. 4): the subgroup of `PSL(2, ℝ)` generated by the classes of these three matrices. -/
noncomputable def K : Subgroup (Matrix.ProjectiveSpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) (⊤ : Subring ℝ)) :=
  Subgroup.closure {QuotientGroup.mk kTrans, QuotientGroup.mk kInv, QuotientGroup.mk kDil}

/-! ### Orders, actions on finite sequences, and the presentations' relators -/

/-- The lexicographic order of p. 3: `s <_lex t` if `t ⊂ s` (a proper initial part), or `s` and `t`
are incompatible and `s(i) < t(i)` at the least `i` with `s(i) ≠ t(i)`. -/
def LexLt (s t : Seq) : Prop :=
  (t <+: s ∧ t ≠ s) ∨
    (Incompatible s t ∧ ∃ i : ℕ, s[i]? = some false ∧ t[i]? = some true ∧ ∀ j < i, s[j]? = t[j]?)

/-- `s.g = t` for `g ∈ SeqGroup` and finite sequences `s`, `t`: `g` maps `s⌢ξ` to `t⌢ξ` for every
infinite sequence `ξ`. -/
def FinAct (g : SeqGroup) (s t : Seq) : Prop := ∀ ξ : Stream' Bool, MulOpposite.unop g (s ++ₛ ξ) = t ++ₛ ξ

/-- The infinite sequence `ξ` is **eventually constant**. -/
def EventuallyConst (ξ : Stream' Bool) : Prop := ∃ N b, ∀ n ≥ N, ξ n = b

open FreeGroup in
/-- The relations (1) and (2) of p. 6, in the generators `x_s` alone (`of s` stands for `x_s`). -/
def RX : Set (FreeGroup Seq) :=
  {r | (∃ s, r = of s * of s * (of (s ++ [false]) * of s * of (s ++ [true]))⁻¹) ∨
    ∃ s t t', xFin s t = some t' ∧ r = of t * of s * (of s * of t')⁻¹}

/-- The generators in `S0`. -/
abbrev GenS0 := {g : Gen // g.InS0}

/-- `R0` as relators in the generators `S0` alone. -/
def R0S : Set (FreeGroup GenS0) := FreeGroup.map Subtype.val ⁻¹' R0

/-- The generators `a`, `b`, `c` of the three-generator presentation (p. 7). -/
inductive ABC
  | a | b | c
  deriving DecidableEq

open FreeGroup in
/-- The nine relations of p. 7 in `a`, `b`, `c`, as relators `lhs · rhs⁻¹` (words multiplied left to
right), with the fourth and ninth as the translations of `y₁₀x₀₁ = x₀₁y₁₀` and
`y₁₀ = x₁₀y₁₀₀y₁₀₁₀⁻¹y₁₀₁₁` through the definitions of p. 6. -/
def nineRels : Set (FreeGroup ABC) :=
  let a := of ABC.a; let b := of ABC.b; let c := of ABC.c
  { b * a⁻¹ ^ 2 * b * a * (a⁻¹ * b * a * b * a⁻¹)⁻¹,
    b * a⁻¹ * a⁻¹ ^ 2 * b * a ^ 2 * (a⁻¹ ^ 2 * b * a ^ 2 * b * a⁻¹)⁻¹,
    c * a ^ 2 * b⁻¹ * a⁻¹ * (a ^ 2 * b⁻¹ * a⁻¹ * c)⁻¹,
    c * (a * b ^ 2 * a⁻¹ * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹ * a⁻¹) * ((a * b ^ 2 * a⁻¹ * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹ * a⁻¹) * c)⁻¹,
    c * a⁻¹ * b * a * (a⁻¹ * b * a * c)⁻¹,
    c * a⁻¹ ^ 2 * b * a ^ 2 * (a⁻¹ ^ 2 * b * a ^ 2 * c)⁻¹,
    c * a * c * a⁻¹ * (a * c * a⁻¹ * c)⁻¹,
    c * a ^ 2 * c * a⁻¹ ^ 2 * (a ^ 2 * c * a⁻¹ ^ 2 * c)⁻¹,
    c * (b ^ 2 * a⁻¹ * b⁻¹ * a * c * a⁻¹ * b * c⁻¹ * a⁻¹ * c * a * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹)⁻¹ }

/-! ### `μ`-amenable equivalence relations (p. 4) -/

/-- `E` is **`μ`-amenable** (p. 4): after discarding a `μ`-null measurable set `N`, `E` is the orbit
equivalence relation of an action of `ℤ` on the rest, here by a Borel automorphism `T` of
`X \ N` (the powers of `T`). -/
def IsMuAmenable {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] (μ : MeasureTheory.Measure X)
    (E : Set (X × X)) : Prop :=
  ∃ N : Set X, MeasurableSet N ∧ μ N = 0 ∧ ∃ T : (Nᶜ : Set X) ≃ᵐ (Nᶜ : Set X),
    ∀ x y : (Nᶜ : Set X), ((x : X), (y : X)) ∈ E ↔ ∃ n : ℤ, (T.toEquiv ^ n) x = y

end LodhaMoore


